  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/markiana_nigripinnis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Markiana nigripinnis is one of the most unusual tetras. It grows to a common size of 12 cm, rarely 15 cm total length. It is easy to keep.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/markiana_nigripinnis_en/",
      "html": "<p>The species inhabits the river basins of the Paraná, Paraguay and the Marmoré (Argentina, Bolivia, Brazil, Paraguay). The specimens imported originate from Argentina.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/markiana_nigripinnis_en/",
      "html": "<p>As it is a relatively large and lively fish that must be kept in schools, one should have a large tank to keep them.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/markiana_nigripinnis_en/",
      "html": "<p>Due to the southern subtropical region where the fish occurs it is not necessary to install a heater in the tank. During summertime the fish can be kept even in garden ponds. Water conditions are of no great importance.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/markiana_nigripinnis_en/",
      "html": "<p>It is an omnivorous species that needs to be fed with plant material, too.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/markiana_nigripinnis_en/",
      "html": "<p>It must be kept in schools. As M. nigripinnis is a hardy and not shy species, it is an ideal companion for large cichlids like Geophagus brasiliensis, for example.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/markiana_nigripinnis_en/",
      "html": "<p>There are specimens with deep orange, black bordered anal fins (males?) and specimens with light orange anal fins without a black border (females?).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/markiana_nigripinnis_en/",
      "html": "<p>The body colour is blue-green-metallic-silvery, with a black spot on the base of the caudal fin; a humeral spot is present, but not always visible. Along the horizontal scale rows are black zigzag lines. The most obvious feature is the very long anal fin, which has a brilliant orange colour. Specimens brought in in autumn show extraordinary brilliant colours.</p>"
    }
  ],
